  "diagnosis_text": "### Diagnosis\n\nThe slowdown is localized to **s2 port 1 \u2192 s1 port 1**, specifically the **egress queue on s2 port 1**.\n\nEvidence:\n\n- `h1 \u2192 h5`: only **0.20 Mbit/s**, with high/variable RTT averaging **123 ms**, but no ICMP loss.\n- `h1 \u2192 h3`, which uses `s4\u2013s2\u2013s5` and avoids the suspect link: **33.6 Gbit/s**.\n- `h7 \u2192 h5`, which stays on the s3 side: **33.0 Gbit/s**.\n- `h3 \u2192 h5`, which crosses `s2\u2013s1\u2013s3`: severely impaired.\n- On **s2 port 1**:\n  - transmit rate held near **1.0 Mbit/s**\n  - **13.6 KB / 8 packets** queued\n  - **357 queue overlimits in two seconds**\n  - no interface errors or drops\n- The neighboring ports on s1 and s3 merely carry the resulting ~1 Mbit/s and show no backlog or overlimits.\n\nAll required links are active, so this is not a link-down or forwarding-path problem. The evidence strongly indicates an **egress rate limiter/shaping qdisc or severe queue contention on `s2-eth1`**, toward s1. Inspect the traffic-control/qdisc configuration and any configured bandwidth limit on that interface. I cannot change it from here.",
